p.src:
The time derivative of Equation  is $\partial_t\psi=i\omega\psi$, while $\frac{\partial^2\psi}{\partial x^2}
  =-\frac{p^2}{\hbar^2}\psi.$ The free Schrödinger equation therefore requires $-\hbar\omega=\frac{p^2}{2m},
  \qquad
  \omega=-\frac{p^2}{2m\hbar}.$ The negative value is a consequence of having written the time factor as $e^{+i\omega t}$. In the conventional positive-frequency notation, $\boxed{
  \psi_p(x,t)
  =
  \exp\left[
    \frac{i}{\hbar}
    \left(px-\frac{p^2}{2m}t\right)
  \right],}$ so $E=p^2/(2m)$ and the time factor is $e^{-iEt/\hbar}$.

